[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last fork that matters, then I'll write the design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m who decides the filename — the caller, or reportkit. This matters because the caller is cron, and cron
  reruns.

  - [1mExplicit [38;5;153m--out PATH[39m (required)[0m — caller names the file; reportkit writes exactly there. Simple, testable, no
    surprises. Cron line carries the date-templated path. Costs: the cron entry gets slightly longer.
  - [1m[38;5;153m--out-dir DIR[39m, filename derived[0m — reportkit writes [38;5;153mbilling-YYYY-MM-DD.csv[39m. Buys consistent naming across
    environments. Costs: the naming scheme becomes a contract, and anything looking for the file has to know the
    convention.
  - [1m[38;5;153m--out PATH[39m optional, default stdout[0m — [38;5;153mexport --format json[39m with no [38;5;153m--out[39m pipes to stdout; [38;5;153m--out[39m writes a file. Buys
    pipeline friendliness for free. Costs: a near-duplicate of [38;5;153msummarize[39m's job, and the "writes a file" framing gets
    fuzzier.

  Paired with that: on rerun, [1moverwrite by default[0m (idempotent — a re-run for the same day replaces the file) versus
  [1mrefuse unless [38;5;153m--force[0m (safe, but a cron retry then fails loudly at 02:00).

  [1mRecommendation: required [38;5;153m--out PATH[39m, overwrite by default.[0m The caller already knows the date it asked for, so it can
  name the file; and a nightly job that can't safely re-run for the same day is a job that pages someone.
